# @fate/tournament — the selection mechanism over the Bauchladen.
# The substrate-decl form of the cache-coherent selector that browses
# the typed display @fate has accumulated and emits a refined
# selection.
#
# === The recognition (#104 chain P4, 2026-06-30) ===
#
# This shard is the fourth tier of the @bauchladen ← @autopoietic ←
# @fate dependency chain per canonical spec at `docs/specs/
# bauchladen-autopoietic-fate.md` (Mara `4575340`, 2726 lines). Tier
# P1 (@bauchladen) landed at `66e1ab8`; tier P2 (@autopoietic) landed
# at `78edaa6`; tier P3 (@fate) landed at `fdcba31`; THIS shard
# lands tier P4 — the sub-prism under @fate whose selection mechanism
# IS itself an @fate inference (autopoietic recursion: @fate
# operating on @fate's own accumulated outputs).
#
# Per spec §6 the tournament is the SELECTION mechanism over the
# Bauchladen tray; it browses prior @fate/algebra/* crystals and
# refines the inference's dice roll based on prior selections. The
# pattern unfolds in four steps:
#
#   1. @fate produces an initial candidate set via `roll(q)`.
#   2. The tournament reads the Bauchladen tray for prior crystals
#      at the same altitude.
#   3. The tournament evaluates the candidates against the prior
#      crystals (by selecting candidates that match patterns in
#      successful prior crystals, by excluding candidates that match
#      patterns in failed prior crystals).
#   4. The tournament emits a refined selection.
#
# Witnesses for the chain's #104 candidacy at this tier:
#   - Canonical spec at `docs/specs/bauchladen-autopoietic-fate.md`
#     §6 (the @fate/tournament sub-prism shape this shard discharges);
#     §6.1 (browses-the-Bauchladen mechanism — the four-step pattern);
#     §6.2 (selection IS itself an @fate inference — the autopoietic
#     recursion); §6.3 (cache-hit / cache-miss operational modes —
#     the BEAM :ets analogue at the substrate altitude); §6.4 (the
#     substrate-decl shape lifts the boot-altitude precedent).
#   - The third-tier landing at `shards/fate.mirror` (`fdcba31`); the
#     family-root the tournament sub-prism extends; the `roll(space,
#     hole)` action this shard's selector consumes; the `dice_roll`
#     carrier this shard's `select` emits as cache-miss path output.
#   - The second-tier landing at `shards/autopoietic.mirror`
#     (`78edaa6`); the `tick_action.candidates` carrier the tournament
#     selects ACROSS; the fold-back permission the tournament inherits
#     transitively via @fate.
#   - The first-tier landing at `shards/bauchladen.mirror` (`66e1ab8`);
#     the tray the tournament browses; the `crystal.provenance` field
#     the tournament's `selection_rule` reads to rank candidates by
#     producing context.
#   - The orphaned boot-floor precursor `boot/std/fate/tournament.
#     mirror` (killed in spring-clean per Taut scout `27c8592`): the
#     `tournament(rules, [hole]) -> [resolution]` declaration; the
#     `rule = greedy | beam(u64) | elite(u64) | halving(u64) |
#     tabu(u64) | anneal(f64) | ucb(f64)` sum; the `compose(rule,
#     rule) -> rule` associative composition; the `candidates(hole)`
#     five-ganglion body. THIS shard is the discharge home: the
#     orphan's signature lifts to substrate-decl altitude here; the
#     rule vocabulary is preserved; the five-ganglion body is already
#     preserved at `shards/optics/source/ganglion/*.mirror` per
#     recognition #58.
#   - The Q5 cache-HIT/cache-MISS pattern at `docs/specs/mosaic-
#     store-cache-invariants.md` §3.4.1: same operational shape (cache
#     lookup over content-addressed graph; hit path is O(1) lookup;
#     miss path is fresh computation + add to graph). The substrate's
#     build cache and @fate's tournament are TWO instances of one
#     substrate pattern at TWO altitudes — build altitude in
#     mosaic-store-cache-invariants, inference altitude here. The
#     parallel is structural, not analogical.
#
# === The autopoietic recursion: @fate operating on @fate's own
#     accumulated outputs ===
#
# Per spec §6.2: the tournament's selection IS itself an @fate
# inference. This is the deepest form of the autopoietic fold-back
# the chain has been building toward: not just @fate consulting its
# own prior outputs (which @fate's family-root admission via `in
# @autopoietic` already guarantees), but @fate selecting WHICH
# selection rule to apply, where the selection-rule choice is itself
# a dice roll within the restricted state space of prior selection
# rules that worked, where THOSE prior rules are themselves crystals
# in the tray that earlier @fate inferences emitted.
#
# The recursion is structural and has NO BOTTOM:
#
#   - @fate's `roll(space, hole)` produces candidates from the
#     restricted state space.
#   - @fate/tournament's `select(query)` refines those candidates by
#     consulting prior selections in the tray.
#   - The refined selection becomes a crystal in the tray (per the
#     cache-miss `add` path).
#   - The next @fate `roll(space, hole)` may consult THIS crystal
#     (autopoietic fold-back at @fate altitude).
#   - The next @fate/tournament `select(query)` may refine
#     DIFFERENTLY based on the new tray contents (autopoietic
#     fold-back at @fate/tournament altitude).
#
# The Lawvere fixed-point condition holds at the SYSTEM level (per
# spec §3.4): some tick will produce a tournament selection that, when
# fed back as input, produces a tournament selection with the same
# OID. The substrate exhibits the fixed point CONSTRUCTIVELY — once
# the tray accumulates enough crystals that the next `select` returns
# a cache hit with byte-equal OID to the prior crystal, the fixed
# point has been exhibited.
#
# === Cache-hit (Bauchladen lookup) vs cache-miss (fresh inference
#     + add to Bauchladen + return) — the BEAM :ets pattern at
#     inference altitude ===
#
# Per spec §6.3: the tournament's selection has two operational
# modes that the type surface MUST disambiguate:
#
#   Mode          Operational path                          Cost
#   -----------   --------------------------------------    --------
#   cache_hit     browse tray → match prior crystal →       O(1)
#                 return the prior crystal as selection;
#                 tray NOT modified
#   cache_miss    fresh @fate.roll(space, hole) →           O(inference)
#                 evaluate against tournament rule →
#                 crystallize new candidate →
#                 add to tray → return new selection
#
# The cache-hit / cache-miss distinction is operationally important:
# cache hits are O(1) (a tray lookup); cache misses are O(inference)
# (running @fate's optical machinery per recognition #58). The
# substrate's intelligence emerges from the cache-hit RATE growing
# over time — early in the substrate's life, every query is a cache
# miss; over time, the tray accumulates enough crystals that most
# queries hit; the substrate becomes FASTER as it accumulates history.
#
# This is the substrate-decl form of what BEAM's `:ets` table provides
# operationally: a content-addressed cache. The substrate's equivalent
# is the Bauchladen tray with the tournament as the cache-coherent
# selector. The analogy is not coincidental — @spectral's BEAM-on-
# mirror operational model (per `shards/spectral.mirror`'s BEAM
# section) already uses :ets-like patterns at the runtime altitude;
# the substrate-decl admission of @fate/tournament makes the pattern
# nameable at the family-root altitude.
#
# Same shape as Q5's mosaic(@store) cache lookup at the build
# altitude per `docs/specs/mosaic-store-cache-invariants.md` §3.4.1:
# the cache-coherent selector pattern recurs across altitudes
# (build / inference / spectral-metalogue); the shape is a substrate
# primitive, not a per-altitude reinvention.
#
# === The boot/std/fate/tournament.mirror migration: this shard is
#     the discharge home ===
