# === Prior art: Connes spectral triples + Mesland correspondences;
#     candidates ARE morphisms in the Mesland category ===
#
# Per `docs/specs/spectral-metalogue.md` `16f4564` §3 + recognition
# #58 + recognition #100: the substrate's spectral triples form a
# Mesland category (Mesland 2013, arXiv:1304.3802) whose objects are
# spectral triples and whose morphisms are unbounded KK-cycles. The
# category structure makes cross-triple operations nameable.
#
# The tournament's CANDIDATES ARE MORPHISMS in the Mesland category.
# At each tournament round, the candidate set the tournament browses
# is a set of typed KK-cycle morphisms between spectral triples; the
# tournament's `select(query)` IS the categorical selection of the
# winning morphism. The autopoietic recursion at the categorical
# altitude: the selected morphism becomes part of the substrate's
# accumulated morphism graph; future tournament rounds consult that
# graph to rank candidates by historical composition behavior.
#
# Three altitudes of the same selection mechanism:
#
#   Altitude              Tournament's role                  Witness
#   -------------------   --------------------------------   ----------
#   Optical               Fabry-Perot mode-selection across  #58
#                         the gain medium's mode catalog
#   Substrate-decl        Tournament-rule-driven selection   this shard
#                         across the @bauchladen tray
#   Spectral-metalogue    Mesland-category morphism          #100
#                         selection across KK-cycle space
#
# === No operational sibling THIS tick ===
#
# Per [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]] no `fate/src/tournament.rs`
# companion or per-rule sub-shard (greedy/beam/elite/halving/tabu/
# anneal/ucb) lands at this tick. The substrate ALREADY has the
# operational machinery: the fate/ runtime crate at the runtime
# altitude; the five-ganglion optical-source shards at the optical
# altitude; the @bauchladen tray at the storage altitude. This shard
# names the sub-prism's substrate-decl contract so downstream shards
# can write `in @fate/tournament` and `requires tournament_well_
# formed(my_round)` rather than re-state the cache-coherent selection
# obligation per consumer. The per-rule sub-shards (`shards/fate/
# tournament/{greedy,beam,elite,halving,tabu,anneal,ucb}.mirror`)
# follow when consumers pull per spec §6.4.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
#   Tournament selection      Goldberg & Deb 1991 ("A comparative
#                             analysis of selection schemes used in
#                             genetic algorithms", *FOGA-1* 69-93).
#                             The original tournament-selection
#                             vocabulary (rule families: tournament,
#                             rank, fitness-proportional).
#   Beam search               Lowerre & Reddy 1976 ("The Harpy speech
#                             recognition system", CMU PhD); Reddy
#                             1976 (*Proc. IEEE* 64(4):501-531). The
#                             beam(k) rule's ancestor; bounded-width
#                             best-first search.
#   Successive halving        Karnin, Koren, Somekh 2013 ("Almost
#                             optimal exploration in multi-armed
#                             bandits", *ICML 2013*); Jamieson &
#                             Talwalkar 2016 ("Non-stochastic best
#                             arm identification and hyperparameter
#                             optimization", *AISTATS 2016*). The
#                             halving(eta) rule's ancestor.
#   Tabu search               Glover 1986 ("Future paths for integer
#                             programming and links to artificial
#                             intelligence", *Computers & OR* 13(5):
#                             533-549). The tabu(tenure) rule's
#                             ancestor.
#   Simulated annealing       Kirkpatrick, Gelatt, Vecchi 1983
#                             ("Optimization by simulated annealing",
#                             *Science* 220(4598):671-680). The
#                             anneal(T) rule's ancestor.
#   UCB / multi-armed bandit  Auer, Cesa-Bianchi, Fischer 2002
#                             ("Finite-time analysis of the multi-
#                             armed bandit problem", *Machine Learning*
#                             47(2-3):235-256). The ucb(c) rule's
#                             ancestor.
#   Cache hit / cache miss    Smith 1982 ("Cache memories", *ACM
#                             Computing Surveys* 14(3):473-530). The
#                             classical formalization of the cache-
#                             coherent-selector pattern this shard's
#                             cache_hit / cache_miss disambiguation
#                             specializes.
#   Spectral triples          Connes 1985; 1995. The (A, H, D)
#                             framework; the tournament selects
#                             within A at the morphism altitude.
#   Mesland category          Mesland 2013 (arXiv:1304.3802). The
#                             category of spectral triples with
#                             KK-cycle morphisms; the tournament's
#                             candidate set IS a typed subset of
#                             the morphism collection between two
#                             spectral triples.
#   BEAM :ets                 Erlang/OTP ETS module (Armstrong 1996+).
#                             The content-addressed cache the
#                             substrate's @bauchladen + this shard's
#                             tournament jointly recapitulate at the
#                             substrate-decl altitude.
#
